  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "What is the sum of the integer solutions of the inequality \\(x^2-5x+6\\leq 0\\)?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "5",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Factoring lets us determine the sign from the two factors:\n\\[\nx^2-5x+6=(x-2)(x-3).\n\\]\nFor \\(2<x<3\\), the factors have opposite signs, so their product is negative. At \\(x=2\\) or \\(x=3\\), the product is zero. For \\(x<2\\), both factors are negative; for \\(x>3\\), both are positive. Thus the product is positive outside the interval. The solution set is \\(2\\leq x\\leq 3\\). The integer solutions are 2 and 3, and their sum is 5.",
